# nftables on a Linux router in front of the VPN appliance (203.0.113.11).
# A stop-gap until the admin interface moves to the management network.
table inet edge {
    set admin_net {
        type ipv4_addr
        flags interval
        elements = { 10.99.0.0/24 }          # management network only
    }
    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority filter; policy accept;
        # admin page, SSH and SNMP on the appliance: admin network only
        ip daddr 203.0.113.11 tcp dport { 22, 8443 } ip saddr != @admin_net drop
        ip daddr 203.0.113.11 udp dport 161 ip saddr != @admin_net drop
        # the VPN portal on 443 stays reachable from anywhere
    }
}

blocks.md:
**Hardening the management plane further** (CCCS ITSM.80.101):

- phishing-resistant MFA for all administrative access;
- central authentication with role-based access, but CCCS calls it "critical to segregate edge devices from an organization's corporate AD forest" (a forest is the outermost Active Directory container, holding all of a company's domains), because linking them to the main identity store widens the attack surface;
- alerts on successful administrative logons, configuration changes and hardware changes;
- two people review every configuration change.

CCCS adds a fair counterpoint: vendors can harden devices so that exposed management interfaces stay safe: "This is a solved problem, and customers should demand vendors harden their devices". Until your vendor proves that, keep the door shut.

